        "title": "Only a game",
        "text": "A  Software that can identify the important events in live TV sports coverage should soon be able to put together programmes of highlights without any human help. When this technology becomes commercially available, it will save millions in editing costs.\n\nB  Picking out the key moments from a game — whether it is snooker, rugby, baseball, football or basketball — takes a great deal of human labour at present. As the footage streams into a TV station or an outside-broadcast truck, someone has to watch the action and keep notes on what happens and when. Only after that are the clips found and put together to make a highlights package, which will probably amount to less than five minutes of viewing per game when it is finally broadcast.\n\nC  However, because sports follow fixed rules and take place in predictable locations, computers ought to be able to pick out the key pieces of play and string them together. Anil Kokaram and his colleagues at Trinity College in Dublin, Ireland, are among the research teams trying to turn this idea into reality. They have decided to analyse table-based ball games such as snooker and pool. These are the sports that a computer should find relatively easy to handle, as the action is slow, the lighting is fairly consistent and cameras mostly film from fixed positions.\n\nD  The Trinity team uses the edges of the table and the positions of the pockets to work out where the balls are on the table. The rules of the game are programmed into the software, so it can follow the moving balls and work out what has happened. For example, if a ball approaches a pocket and then disappears from view, the program assumes it has been potted. By working out how to detect foul shots — when a player hits the wrong ball — the team hopes to find a way to create an exciting highlights package for the sport.\n\nE  Until recently, the chances of producing similar software for football were not high. Football involves a far greater number of moving objects (22 players and a ball) on a playing field whose appearance can change with the weather and the lighting, so it had been proving an impossible challenge for developers. But then Carlo Colombo and his colleagues at the University of Florence in Italy began to approach the task in a different way. They have found that they can put together highlights from footage without tracking either the ball or the moving players. Instead, they have looked at where the players stand in set pieces. Their software detects the position of the pitch markings in a shot to work out which area is in the frame. Then, by checking the positions the players take up in relation to the markings, the software can decide whether a player is about to take a penalty, a free kick or a corner, and whether a goal is scored as a result.\n\nF  The Florence team has not yet worked out how to make the computer decide when a goal is scored in open play. However, Ahmet Ekin, a computer scientist from the University of Rochester in New York, may be close to solving that problem. He has designed software that looks for a particular sequence of camera shots to work out whether a goal has been scored. For example, close-ups of players often show a break in play when something important has happened, and slow-motion footage is another useful clue. Ekin also includes sound analysis, so it is possible that the software could listen for the commentator's excited shouts of 'Goooaal!'\n\nG  A Japanese electronics company has been testing a simple highlights package that can cut an hour of American football down to around 14 minutes, and an hour of baseball down to a few minutes. In the end, the company wants to develop highlights software for a new generation of video recorders, which would allow people to make their own sports highlights packages from the comfort of their living rooms."
